WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.535
A plan describes a desired network.

2
00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:08.963
Programming decides which projects advance, when they advance, and how they are paid for.

3
00:00:08.963 --> 00:00:15.855
The source chapter presents programming as the bridge between long-range vision and real construction or service.

4
00:00:15.855 --> 00:00:27.199
Because available money is limited, agencies prioritize, fund, schedule, and document projects in instruments such as a transportation improvement program or capital improvement program.

5
00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:28.785
Fiscal constraint matters.

6
00:00:28.785 --> 00:00:34.640
A list of desirable projects is not a program unless expected revenues and costs can support it.

7
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:41.532
Funding sources also bring rules about eligible uses, local match, timing, environmental review, and performance.

8
00:00:41.532 --> 00:00:49.522
A project can rank highly yet fail to advance because it is not ready, lacks a complete funding plan, or depends on another agency.

9
00:00:49.522 --> 00:00:51.839
Evaluation begins before construction.

10
00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:54.889
Agencies define criteria and compare alternatives.

11
00:00:54.889 --> 00:01:04.648
Common measures include safety, accessibility, mobility, system condition, reliability, environmental effects, economic effects, equity, and cost effectiveness.

12
00:01:04.648 --> 00:01:06.355
A measure must fit the goal.

13
00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:12.088
Average vehicle speed does not directly measure whether residents without cars can reach work.

14
00:01:12.088 --> 00:01:15.687
Ridership does not by itself describe passenger experience.

15
00:01:15.687 --> 00:01:22.335
A benefit-cost result depends on assumptions about time, discounting, risk, and which effects were monetized.

16
00:01:22.335 --> 00:01:28.312
After implementation, performance monitoring asks whether the project delivered what was promised.

17
00:01:28.312 --> 00:01:34.655
Evaluation should preserve the baseline, record changes in context, and distinguish output from outcome.

18
00:01:34.655 --> 00:01:36.973
Miles of lane installed are an output.

19
00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:41.364
Reduced severe collisions or shorter reliable travel times are outcomes.

20
00:01:41.364 --> 00:01:42.718
Torrance application.

21
00:01:42.718 --> 00:01:48.007
This section is a separate educational example based on the Torrance General Plan.

22
00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:56.649
Imagine a candidate corridor program containing signal work, sidewalk repairs, bus-stop access improvements, and a bicycle connection.

23
00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:03.615
A transparent local process would first state the goals, then score each component using published criteria.

24
00:02:03.615 --> 00:02:13.224
Safety need, access to schools and jobs, network connectivity, project readiness, lifecycle cost, and availability of outside funds could all matter.

25
00:02:13.224 --> 00:02:20.190
The program should show total cost, funding source, responsible department, dependencies, and expected year.

26
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:25.478
If one grant pays only for construction, design and maintenance still need owners.

27
00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:33.153
If a lower-cost access improvement serves people who have few alternatives, an equity criterion may change the ranking.

28
00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:37.023
The City source does not supply this hypothetical scorecard.

29
00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.667
The example applies the chapter's method.

30
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:42.892
It does not recommend a Torrance capital decision.

31
00:02:42.892 --> 00:02:47.665
Current budgets, plans, grant terms, and public records would be required.

32
00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:59.145
